---
/**
 * THE ICON RAIL — the console's only navigation.
 *
 * OBOS is an ops console rather than a map with panels floating over it, and this
 * narrow column is the whole of its navigation. Explore and Compare used to be
 * tabs in the stage header; they are now two of the five places the rail can take
 * you. Everything that used to be a second navigation is gone, because one
 * navigation is easier to learn than two.
 *
 * ITS WIDTH IS NOW A STATE, AND IT IS WRITTEN ONCE. The rail expands to show its
 * labels and collapses to the icon strip, so the column is two numbers rather
 * than one — and the old arrangement could not hold two. Explore took a fallback
 * spelled inside this file while PairedBench.astro declared a literal of its own,
 * because the bench's rail is `position:fixed` and the bench is padded past it by
 * `calc(var(--rail) + …)`: a value it cannot read from a fallback it never names.
 * Two numbers with a test comparing them was tolerable for a constant; for a state
 * it would have been two numbers per route in three files, agreeing at both ends.
 *
 * So both widths and the CURRENT one are declared here, on `:root`, and every
 * reader on both routes reads the same property. `:root` rather than `.rail`
 * because custom properties inherit DOWNWARDS: a width published on the rail
 * could never reach the padding whose whole job is to clear it. This is the one
 * thing this component declares outside itself, and it is declared where a
 * page-level singleton belongs.
 *
 * THE COLLAPSE IS REMEMBERED, NOT ROUTED. shell/layout-state.ts owns the key, the
 * attribute and the pre-paint script, and writes down why the preference is in
 * localStorage rather than in the URL.
 *
 * BELOW THE STAGE'S OWN BREAKPOINT THE RAIL IS COLLAPSED WHATEVER WAS STORED, and
 * the chevron goes with it. At that width HeatMapStage.astro already drops the
 * sidebar entirely; a 172px navigation column over what is left of the map would
 * be the same trade made worse, and a control that cannot change anything is one
 * this project keeps deleting.
 *
 * THE RAIL CARRIES THE MODE, AND THE VERBS ARE MIXED ON PURPOSE:
 *
 *   Map        the Explore route, and the toolbox pane once you are on it
 *   Layers     swaps the sidebar pane
 *   Analysis   the Compare route, and the paired bench's pane once you are on it
 *   Reports    swaps the sidebar pane
 *   Scenarios  swaps the sidebar pane
 *
 * Two of the five change the URL and four of them change what the sidebar shows.
 * Every ops console works this way, and the alternative — a rail for routes and a
 * second control for panes — is the two-navigations problem again.
 *
 * Map and Analysis are SYMMETRIC: each is a route, each owns the pane that route
 * renders, and each is a plain link from the other page. Whichever of the two you
 * are standing on is a button; the other is the way across.
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * TWO PROPS, BECAUSE THERE ARE TWO QUESTIONS. `route` is WHICH PAGE THIS IS;
 * `pane` is WHICH SIDEBAR BODY IS SHOWING. The first version of this component
 * conflated them into one `active`, and flagged the consequence itself: with
 * `active="layers"` on the Explore route, Map still rendered as an `<a>` pointing
 * at the URL the reader was already standing on — the self-link spec 1 removed,
 * reintroduced by the act of opening a pane.
 *
 * Split, the rule is simple and holds in every combination: A SECTION IS A LINK
 * ONLY WHEN IT NAVIGATES SOMEWHERE ELSE. The section whose id equals `route` has
 * its href taken away by `items` below, so no arm of the template can render it as
 * an anchor, whatever pane happens to be open.
 *
 * The two facts are then stated separately in the output, because they ARE
 * separate: `aria-current="page"` on the route (a fact about the document) and
 * `aria-pressed` on the pane sections (a fact about the sidebar). A screen reader
 * hears both, without either having to stand in for the other.
 *
 * `pane` LIVES IN MEMORY, NOT IN THE URL. The switcher in
 * scripts/climate-engine/shell/console-shell.ts owns that decision and writes down
 * why; this prop is only the state the SERVER renders first.
 *
 * ONE BRAND, ONE PLACE. The mark at the top is the link home. The stage's top bar
 * used to carry a second one directly to its right — composed, that was two brands
 * eight pixels apart, both going to `/`. The top bar kept the breadcrumb and the
 * readout; the mark moved here, where the navigation already is.
 *
 * NO SETTINGS CONTROL. The first version shipped one, inert, and said so. Nothing
 * exists for it to open, and a control that does nothing is the defect this project
 * has repeatedly deleted — so it is deleted rather than wired to a panel that would
 * have had to be invented to justify it. The chevron beside the wordmark is the
 * one control here that is not a section, and it is wired in console-shell.ts
 * before it is rendered.
 *
 * NO SECTION HEADERS. The mockup grouped the five under "Console" and "Output".
 * Five rows do not need to be grouped — the headers would add two more strings to
 * keep out of the tab order and two more surfaces to hold to the contrast floor,
 * to divide a list short enough to be read at a glance.
 *
 * The icons are the founder-approved set from the shell mockup, path data copied
 * verbatim.
 */
import { DEFAULT_AREA_PATH } from '../../../scripts/climate-engine/scope/paths.ts';
import {
  LAYOUT_PREPAINT,
  RAIL_TOGGLE_ID,
} from '../../../scripts/climate-engine/shell/layout-state.ts';

/** The five places the rail can be. Exported for callers to name one. */
export type RailSection = 'map' | 'layers' | 'analysis' | 'reports' | 'scenarios';

interface Props {
  /**
   * The section the page IS. The one that renders as a button rather than as a
   * link to itself, and the one that carries `aria-current="page"`.
   */
  route: RailSection;
  /**
   * The sidebar body showing right now. Independent of `route`: you can stand on
   * Explore with the Layers pane open, which is the case the single-prop version
   * could not express without lying about one of the two.
   */
  pane: RailSection;
  /**
   * Where Map goes. The page knows its own area; the rail does not, and guessing
   * would put a second copy of the default area URL in a second file.
   */
  explorePath?: string;
}

const { route, pane, explorePath = DEFAULT_AREA_PATH } = Astro.props;

/** The compare route, written once. `paths.ts` owns the per-area URLs, not this. */
const COMPARE_PATH = '/heat-map/compare/';

/**
 * THE CHEVRON'S TWO NAMES, AND THEY NAME THE ACTION RATHER THAN THE STATE.
 *
 * "Collapse the navigation rail" says what pressing it will do; "Navigation rail"
 * would leave a reader to infer it from `aria-expanded` alone, and an icon-only
 * button with no name at all is the regression this replaces — five glyphs whose
 * only labels were tooltips, which a touch device never shows and a first-time
 * reader never discovers.
 *
 * BOTH ARE RENDERED, as data attributes on the button. The name has to change when
 * the state does, and the two scripts that change it — the pre-paint one from
 * layout-state.ts and the shell's click handler — would otherwise each carry their
 * own copy of both strings. Written here, read from the DOM, they have one author.
 */
const TOGGLE_LABEL = {
  expanded: 'Collapse the navigation rail',
  collapsed: 'Expand the navigation rail',
} as const;

/**
 * One rail glyph: an optional circle plus a path, both drawn on a 24-unit box.
 *
 * Structured rather than a blob of markup passed to `set:html`, because Astro's
 * scoping hash only lands on elements Astro itself renders — an `<svg>` injected
 * as raw HTML would silently miss `.rail-btn svg` and lose its stroke.
 */
interface Icon {
  readonly circle?: readonly [cx: number, cy: number, r: number];
  readonly d: string;
}

/**
 * WHERE A SECTION'S SIDEBAR BODY IS RENDERED. Not whether it has one — where.
 *
 *   `always`      on every route the rail appears on. Layers, Reports and
 *                 Scenarios are answers ABOUT wherever you are standing, so
 *                 both routes render them.
 *   `own-route`   only on the route of the same name. Map's body is the
 *                 toolbox and Analysis's body is the paired bench; each is a
 *                 plain link from the other page, where its body is not in the
 *                 document at all.
 */
type PaneBody = 'always' | 'own-route';

interface Section {
  readonly id: RailSection;
  readonly label: string;
  /** where it navigates, or null when it navigates nowhere */
  readonly href: string | null;
  /** where its sidebar body is rendered */
  readonly body: PaneBody;
  readonly icon: Icon;
}

/* TWO FIELDS, AND THEY ARE NOT ONE OPINION WRITTEN TWICE. The first version
   carried only `href`, with `href: null` doing double duty as "this one swaps a
   pane" — which was true only while no section did both. Map does both: it is the
   Explore ROUTE from anywhere else, and the toolbox PANE once you are standing on
   it. Layers is the entry that proves they still vary independently: no href, and
   a body on every route.

   THE SECOND FIELD WAS A BOOLEAN AND COULD NOT HOLD THE TRUTH. `analysis: pane
   false` was a fact about the EXPLORE route — Analysis navigates away and owns no
   body there — being read on both routes. Composed onto Compare it made Analysis
   the current page with no pane, and Layers, Reports and Scenarios into buttons
   with `aria-pressed="false"` and nothing behind them: three dead controls on the
   console's only navigation, which is this project's most-deleted defect. Where a
   body is rendered depends on the route, so the field says so. */
const SECTIONS: readonly Section[] = [
  { id: 'map', label: 'Map', href: explorePath, body: 'own-route', icon: {
    d: 'M9 3 3 6v15l6-3 6 3 6-3V3l-6 3-6-3zM9 3v15M15 6v15' } },
  { id: 'layers', label: 'Layers', href: null, body: 'always', icon: {
    d: 'M12 2 2 7l10 5 10-5-10-5zM2 17l10 5 10-5M2 12l10 5 10-5' } },
  { id: 'analysis', label: 'Analysis', href: COMPARE_PATH, body: 'own-route', icon: {
    d: 'M3 3v18h18M7 15l4-5 3 3 5-7' } },
  { id: 'reports', label: 'Reports', href: null, body: 'always', icon: {
    d: 'M14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8zM14 2v6h6M9 13h6M9 17h6' } },
  { id: 'scenarios', label: 'Scenarios', href: null, body: 'always', icon: {
    circle: [12, 12, 9], d: 'M12 8v4l3 2' } },
];

/* WHETHER THIS ROUTE RENDERS THE SECTION'S BODY. The one question `aria-pressed`
   may be written about, and the one the shell script reads back to decide which
   buttons get a pane-swap handler. It reads `route`, never `pane`: a body either
   is or is not in this document, which has nothing to do with which one happens
   to be showing. */
const ownsPane = (s: Section): boolean => s.body === 'always' || s.id === route;

/* THE ONE LINE THAT MAKES THE CURRENT ROUTE A BUTTON. Taking the href away is what
   removes the self-link, and it removes it for every section at once rather than
   leaving each one to remember. The template then only has to ask whether a
   section has somewhere to go — and because this reads `route` and never `pane`,
   opening a pane cannot put the self-link back. */
const items = SECTIONS.map((s) => {
  const current = s.id === route;
  return {
    ...s,
    current,
    /* `undefined`, not `false`, on a section whose body THIS ROUTE does not
       render: `aria-pressed` announces a toggle, and Analysis-on-Explore is not
       one — nor is Map-on-Compare. Astro drops the attribute
       entirely when the value is undefined — and the shell script reads the
       ATTRIBUTE back to learn which sections have panes, so its absence is the
       fact rather than a spelling of "off". The literals are spelled out because
       `aria-pressed` is a string enum, not a boolean attribute: `String(x)` is
       the same two words with the type thrown away. */
    pressed: ownsPane(s) ? (s.id === pane ? 'true' as const : 'false' as const) : undefined,
    href: current ? null : s.href,
  };
});
---
<nav class="rail" aria-label="OBOS sections">
  {/* THE ONE BRAND ON THE PAGE, and the way out of the console. `aria-label`
      rather than `title`: title is not a reliable accessible name — ignored by
      some screen readers, never surfaced on touch — and the logo is a mark, not
      a sentence anyone can act on. The <img> carries alt="" because the anchor
      is already named; a described image inside a labelled link is read twice.

      THE MARK NO LONGER SITS ON A TILE. The "OB" it replaces was ink on a cyan
      gradient, which is what two letters need to read as a mark. The logo is
      itself cyan on transparent, so the same tile would have printed cyan on
      cyan — the plate is dropped and the logo meets the rail's ground directly.
      `eager`, not lazy: it is the topmost thing in the viewport on both routes,
      and deferring it only guarantees a visible pop-in. */}
  <div class="brand">
    <a class="mark" href="/" aria-label="OBOS — Delta Climate Research home">
      <img src="/logo-mark-96.webp" alt="" width="34" height="34" loading="eager" decoding="async" />
      {/* THE WORDMARK IS INSIDE THE LINK AND THE NAME BEGINS WITH IT. A visible
          word whose control announces something else is WCAG 2.5.3 — someone
          saying "OBOS" to a voice driver would be naming a link the browser
          believes is called "Delta Climate Research home". */}
      <b class="wordmark">OBOS</b>
    </a>

    {/* THE COLLAPSE CONTROL. `aria-expanded` states which way the rail is; the
        label states what pressing it does, and console-shell.ts swaps both.
        Server-rendered EXPANDED because that is the default and because it is
        what a reader with no JavaScript will keep — nothing can collapse the
        rail for them, so "expanded" is not an optimistic guess, it is the truth
        about that document. */}
    <button
      type="button"
      class="rail-toggle"
      id={RAIL_TOGGLE_ID}
      data-rail-toggle
      aria-expanded="true"
      aria-label={TOGGLE_LABEL.expanded}
      data-label-expanded={TOGGLE_LABEL.expanded}
      data-label-collapsed={TOGGLE_LABEL.collapsed}
    >
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
        <path d="m14 6-6 6 6 6" />
      </svg>
    </button>
  </div>

  {items.map((s) => s.href === null ? (
    <button
      type="button"
      class="rail-btn"
      data-rail={s.id}
      aria-current={s.current ? 'page' : undefined}
      aria-pressed={s.pressed}
    >
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
        {s.icon.circle && <circle cx={s.icon.circle[0]} cy={s.icon.circle[1]} r={s.icon.circle[2]} />}
        <path d={s.icon.d} />
      </svg>
      <span class="rail-label">{s.label}</span>
    </button>
  ) : (
    <a class="rail-btn" data-rail={s.id} href={s.href}>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
        {s.icon.circle && <circle cx={s.icon.circle[0]} cy={s.icon.circle[1]} r={s.icon.circle[2]} />}
        <path d={s.icon.d} />
      </svg>
      <span class="rail-label">{s.label}</span>
    </a>
  ))}
</nav>

{/* BEFORE THE RAIL IS PAINTED, AND AFTER IT IS PARSED. The stored preference has
    to be on <html> before the first paint or a reader who collapsed the rail
    watches it collapse — and animate — on every load; it has to be after the
    <nav> so the same pass can correct the chevron the server rendered expanded.
    `is:inline` keeps it verbatim and blocking, which is the whole point;
    `data-astro-rerun` runs it again after a ClientRouter swap, because the swap
    copies the incoming document's <html> attributes over ours and this one is
    not in any document the server wrote. A <script> is `display:none` by UA
    default, so it is not a grid or flex item of the console around it. */}
<script is:inline data-astro-rerun set:html={LAYOUT_PREPAINT} />

<style>
  /* SCOPED, NOT is:global. The stage's own block is global for a reason that does
     not extend here: MapLibre injects DOM the stage never rendered and
     heat-map-app.ts re-classes elements at runtime, so Astro's scoping hash cannot
     reach them. Every element in this file is Astro-rendered and static, the hash
     reaches all of it, and the global surface shrinks rather than grows.

     THE ONE EXCEPTION IS THE COLUMN'S WIDTH, and `:global()` inside a SCOPED block
     is the sanctioned way to write it — not a globally-marked block, which would
     take the hash off everything else in the file and, worse, would make the
     `:global()` below ship verbatim for the browser to discard. Astro's compiler
     turns `:global(:root)` here into a bare `:root`; three rules leave this file
     and the other thirty stay scoped.

     Colours are var() throughout, and exactly one hex is written here: the
     resting ink, which is this component's own and belongs to nothing else.
     Task 2's guard fails the build on any hex written more than once in a file,
     comments included. --rail-ground and the rest of the palette are declared in
     HeatMapStage.astro, which is where the tokens live. */

  /* ── THE COLUMN'S WIDTH ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Three declarations, and they are the only place either number is written.

     ON :root BECAUSE CUSTOM PROPERTIES INHERIT DOWNWARDS. The paired bench's rail
     is `position:fixed`, and the bench clears it with
     `padding-inline-start:calc(var(--rail) + …)` — a reader ABOVE the rail in the
     tree. Published on `.rail` the width could reach the rail's own children and
     nothing else, so the bench would need a copy, and a copy that has to track two
     states across two routes is four numbers and a guard hoping they agree.

     COLLAPSED IS THE BASE, EXPANDED IS THE CONDITION, which is the opposite of how
     the design reads and is deliberate. The expanded rail requires two things at
     once — room for it, and a reader who has not asked for it gone — and stating
     that as one rule is what keeps the width, the labels and the chevron from
     disagreeing about which state they are in. It also degrades in the right
     direction: anything that cannot resolve the condition gets the icon strip
     this component shipped before it could expand at all.

     820px IS THE STAGE'S NUMBER. HeatMapStage.astro drops the sidebar entirely at
     `max-width:820px`; `width > 820px` is that condition's exact complement, so
     there is no width at which the rail believes one thing and the console the
     other. A unit test reads both files and compares the two numbers. */
  :global(:root) {
    --rail-collapsed: 58px;
    --rail-expanded: 172px;
    --rail: var(--rail-collapsed);
    /* The rail's own box, inset and bordered. */
    --rail-inset: 10px;
    /* WHERE THE RAIL'S RIGHT EDGE ACTUALLY IS, published because three rules on
       the compare route have to clear it and none of them can see this file's
       margin. `--rail` is the COLUMN; this is the column plus the inset it floats
       by and the hairline that draws it, which is what "clear the rail" means to
       anything sitting beside it.

       Derived rather than typed. The alternative was `calc(var(--rail) + 12px)`
       written in three places — the bench's padding, the mobile padding and the
       sheet's offset — where changing the inset would have moved the rail and left
       all three clearances behind. That is the shape of defect this file already
       carries a scar from: the accent bar kept its own copy of the rail's width
       and floated off the edge the moment the column moved. */
    --rail-edge: calc(var(--rail) + var(--rail-inset) + 2px);
  }

  @media (width > 820px) {
    :global(html:not([data-rail="collapsed"])) { --rail: var(--rail-expanded); }
  }

  /* A PANEL THAT FLOATS, NOT A COLUMN THAT ABUTS.
     The rail used to meet the sidebar edge to edge, separated by one border. Both
     now sit inset with a gap between them and their own rounded corners, which is
     the treatment PairedBench.astro's sidebar already had — so this unifies the
     two routes rather than introducing a third idea.

     THE BORDER GOES ALL THE WAY ROUND NOW, and it is doing real work rather than
     decoration. The rail and the panel share one colour, and the page behind the
     gap is 1.06:1 from it — invisible. What separates them is the SHAPE: the gap
     and the radius, drawn by a hairline. Take the border away and the two panels
     dissolve into the ground they float on. */
  .rail {
    inline-size: var(--rail);
    background: var(--rail-ground);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 12px;
    margin: var(--rail-inset) 0 var(--rail-inset) var(--rail-inset);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 10px;
    padding: 12px 0;
    /* The map beside it relayouts on every frame of this, and MapLibre's
       ResizeObserver reallocates its GL drawing buffer to match — which is the
       reason the SIDEBAR's collapse is instant and says so in HeatMapStage.astro.
       The rail is transitioned anyway, and the difference is that this is one
       gesture rather than a state the reader lives in: 114px over 180ms, once,
       when they press the chevron. */
    transition: inline-size .18s ease;
  }

  /* --rail-ink IS GONE, AND ITS JOB WENT BACK TO --ink.
     It existed for one reason: --faint could not clear 4.5:1 on --rail-ground, so
     a rail that had grown labels needed a lighter value than the token it would
     otherwise have used. --faint has since been raised to clear that floor
     everywhere it is painted, which removed the reason without removing the
     token — two names for one job, and the dimmer of the two would have been the
     one to drift.
     The rail resolves to --ink instead: these are primary navigation labels, so
     the ink tier is where they belonged all along, and --faint would have read
     them as secondary. A unit test recomputes the ratio from the declared values
     rather than trusting the number in this sentence. */

  /* THE BRAND ROW: mark, wordmark, chevron. Collapsed it is a column — the mark
     centred with the chevron beneath it, because the chevron is the ONLY way back
     out of the collapsed rail and hiding it there would be a door that locks. */
  .brand {
    inline-size: calc(var(--rail) - 20px);
    flex: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }

  .mark {
    flex: none;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 9px;
    min-inline-size: 0;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--paper);
    transition: transform .18s;
  }

  /* Sized in CSS as well as in the attributes. The width/height pair reserves the
     box before the file lands, which is what stops the rail jumping; these rules
     are what survive a user stylesheet or a zoom that overrides them. */
  .mark img {
    inline-size: 34px;
    block-size: 34px;
    flex: none;
    object-fit: contain;
  }

  .wordmark {
    display: none;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: .78rem;
    letter-spacing: -.01em;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .mark:hover { transform: scale(1.06); }
  .mark:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--cyan); outline-offset: 3px; }

  .rail-toggle {
    flex: none;
    inline-size: 26px;
    block-size: 26px;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 0;
    background: none;
    color: var(--ink);
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: color .18s, background .18s;
  }

  .rail-toggle svg {
    inline-size: 15px;
    block-size: 15px;
    stroke: currentColor;
    fill: none;
    stroke-width: 1.8;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
    /* Points BACK the way pressing it goes: right to open the rail out when it is
       shut, left to fold it away when it is open. */
    transform: rotate(180deg);
    transition: transform .18s;
  }

  .rail-toggle:hover { color: var(--paper); background: rgb(111 202 214 / .06); }
  .rail-toggle:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--cyan); outline-offset: 2px; }

  /* One class for both arms: a link and a button that look identical, because
     which one you get is a fact about where you already are, not a difference the
     eye should have to resolve.

     ITS WIDTH IS THE COLUMN'S, MINUS THE SAME GUTTER AT BOTH ENDS. That is what
     keeps the accent bar's derivation below true at every width without a second
     rule: the button stays centred in the rail, so half the rail back from the
     button's own centre is the rail's edge, whatever the rail currently is. */
  .rail-btn {
    inline-size: calc(var(--rail) - 20px);
    block-size: 38px;
    flex: none;
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 0;
    background: none;
    color: var(--ink);
    font: inherit;
    font-size: .78rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    text-align: start;
    text-decoration: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: color .18s, background .18s;
  }

  .rail-btn svg {
    inline-size: 19px;
    block-size: 19px;
    flex: none;
    stroke: currentColor;
    fill: none;
    stroke-width: 1.6;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
  }

  /* BRIGHTER ON HOVER, NOT DIMMER. This used to reach for --ink, which is a step
     DOWN from the resting ink the contrast fix installed — so pointing at a row
     would have faded it. */
  .rail-btn:hover { color: var(--paper); background: rgb(111 202 214 / .06); }

  /* A rail you cannot see focus on is unusable by keyboard, and this is the only
     navigation there is. */
  .rail-btn:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--cyan);
    outline-offset: 2px;
    color: var(--paper);
  }

  /* TWO STATES, TWO WEIGHTS, because they are two different facts and the reader
     has to be able to tell them apart. The OPEN PANE is the loud one — cyan and
     the accent bar — because it is what the sidebar is showing right now and what
     a second click will close. The CURRENT ROUTE is the quiet one: a page you are
     already on is worth stating and is not worth shouting, and on the Explore
     route with Layers open the two are different sections. */
  .rail-btn[aria-current="page"] { color: var(--paper); }
  .rail-btn[aria-pressed="true"] { color: var(--cyan); background: rgb(111 202 214 / .1); }

